+# BUG-042 — the conformance check reads the weekly scan as the gate
+
+Found by stage T2's review, round 1 (Review A's ci part, its B1, a REJECT; the bridge part named the same line as a Consider; Review B silent), reproduced there on scratch projects. Read from `bridge/cursor-bridge/conformance-check.py` at origin/main 1f50cdf and shown by the reproduction tests the supervisor wrote first, in `tests/test_bug042.py`, which fail there for the reason given.
+
+**Symptom.** A project's gate with no OSV-Scanner step, beside the weekly `dependency-scan.yml` that release T puts in every project, reads `OK` on the floor `OSV-Scanner in the gate`; so does a gate whose step is guarded by `if:`. Each reads `MISSING` without the weekly file. The weekly file's pinned download also hides the `OSV-Scanner version pinned` NOTE for an unpinned gate step. It bites once the release delivers the file: a calibration would read a gate with no scanner as conformant.
+
+**Confirmed cause.** `main()` reads every workflow under `.github/workflows/` as the gate: `steps` is extended from every one of them and `scanner_lines` is taken from `wf_text`, the join of all of them, so the unguarded OSV-Scanner step of the weekly file alone satisfies the floor. The weekly file is a second workflow, R-052's own word, and answers for no floor of the gate.
+
+**The fix.** A file named `dependency-scan` with the extension `.yml` or `.yaml`, in any case, is left out of the gate's workflows; the scanner rows and the steps the guarded-step test reads take the rest. The other readers stay on every workflow: the actions-pinned, runner, design-detector and command-reference rows on the whole text, and the two Socket hazard rows (`Socket runs without its banner`, `Socket CLI installed without the key`) on every workflow's steps, since a key in the weekly file's log is a key in a log whatever the file is for.
+
+**Round 2: a regression in round 1, from the supervisor's own brief.** Requirement 1 of `handoff/BUG-042.md` made every reader of `steps` gate-only, which blinded the two Socket hazard rows: a Socket step in the weekly file, with its banner on or its key in the install's environment, read as silence where it had read `MISSING`. The security audit reproduced it; Review A called it contrived. REVIEWERS DISAGREE, on correctness and not intent; decided for the fix, as a regression of this diff on a file a builder can write, one list costing nothing, the rows existing for any workflow. `handoff/BUG-042-r2.md` splits the lists.
+
+**Reproduction:** `test_bug042_a_gate_with_no_osv_step_stays_missing_beside_the_weekly_file`, `test_bug042_a_guarded_gate_step_stays_missing_beside_the_weekly_file`, `test_bug042_an_unpinned_gate_step_keeps_its_note_beside_the_weekly_file`, `test_bug042_the_weekly_file_alone_is_no_gate`, `test_bug042_the_weekly_file_is_known_by_its_name_in_either_form`, and for round 2 `test_bug042_a_socket_hazard_in_the_weekly_file_still_reads_missing`.
+
+**Negative cases:** `test_bug042_negative_a_gate_step_beside_the_weekly_file_is_ok`, `test_bug042_negative_an_action_in_the_weekly_file_still_counts_for_the_pin_row`, `test_bug042_the_same_steps_under_another_name_are_a_gate`, and every test of `tests/test_conformance_check.py`.
+
+**Carried in the same fix, each its own cause, from the same review.**
+- The guard's module docstring and `snapshot_rewritten`'s named exit 2 and a hash difference where BUG-041 made a snapshot deleted after it was taken, its hash copy standing, GATE-INTEGRITY; round 2 narrowed the words to what the code does: exit 2 is no copy of the snapshot's hash, and `verify` voids a qualifying launch, `premerge` nothing (Review A's records and briefs parts; S1 and S2 of round 1's read). No code line of that program changed. `test_bug042_the_guard_s_docstrings_name_the_deleted_snapshot`.
+- `intake.md`'s `Layout:` sentence said to record both resolved paths, against rule 54 and TASK-041's relative line (Review A's bridge part, S1). `test_bug042_intake_records_the_layout_as_the_core_does`.
+- `configuration.md`, the configuration guide's order of operations and `cursor-configurator.md` left out the weekly workflow and the boundary log's `.gitignore` line (bridge part, S2). `test_bug042_the_configuration_lists_name_the_weekly_scan`, `test_bug042_the_configurator_gitignores_the_boundary_log`.
+- `cursor-configurator.md`'s stop had no way forward (ci part C2, bridge part C3; both reviewers on each round after it): the stop now checks every value, its shell quotes aside, knows a standing file by either form the conformance check knows and compares it whole, the block filled, says how the supervisor settles each case (an older standing file replaced whole by the increment that moves the pins, any other read and never overwritten unread), never takes a newer floor pin back, reads local gates' value and pin against their local command, and reads R-052's every project with two exceptions (INVENTORY, Decisions). `test_bug042_the_configurator_s_stop_has_a_way_forward`.
+- `evaluate-reports.md` tested the workflow list for a file name that a plain `gh workflow list` does not print (bridge part, C1): it now reads `dependency-scan`. `test_bug042_the_evaluation_reads_the_list_by_what_it_prints`. The guide's item 8 and the evaluation's step 1b name the stop's two exceptions, so a project with no remote is not a finding every Sunday and a wait for a newer pin is not a calibration item (Review A's S3, S5). `test_bug042_the_guide_and_the_evaluation_know_the_stop_s_two_cases`.
+- The tests part's C3: the at-or-after boundary at `review-guard.py:401`, `:367` and `:1047` (lines at 1f50cdf) was unpinned, `<=` surviving both guard test files; `tests/test_bug039.py` now holds a launch in the snapshot's own second from both sides. Its C2: TASK-042's m35 renamed `a-shell-other-than-bash`. Its C4: `--strip` with `--key-file` is exercised together by `test_bug042_negative_a_stripped_suite_launch_gets_its_key_file`.
+
+**What this does and does not hold.** The exclusion is by the file's name, so a copy of the weekly steps under another name reads as a gate, which is the right reading of a workflow nobody named a scan, and a gate renamed `dependency-scan` reads as no gate, which fails closed. It reads no trigger: a workflow under another name with only `workflow_dispatch`, a job-level `if:`, `continue-on-error`, a comment or an `echo` holding the scanner's name still read `OK`, as they do at origin/main, a program lead in `docs/HARDENING.md`. Nothing checks the weekly file against the frozen block, the conformance floor the CHANGELOG names for the maintainer.
